<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mynydd Carn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1081">1081</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Mynydd Carn was fought in 1081 at an unidentified site in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Gruffudd ap Cynan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a> defeated a coalition of Trahaearn ap Caradog of Gwynedd, Caradog ap Gruffudd of Gwent and Morgannwg, and Meilyr ap Rhiwallon, all three of whom were killed. The victory secured Gruffudd's claim to Gwynedd and Rhys's rule of Deheubarth.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle is recorded in a near-contemporary biography of one of its leaders, The History of Gruffydd ap Cynan. Gruffudd, a descendant of the traditional ruling house of Gwynedd, had tried to claim the kingdom in 1075 but had been defeated by Trahaearn ap Caradog and forced to take refuge in Ireland.</p><p>In 1081 he sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> with a force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and Irishmen, landing not in Gwynedd but further south, near St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s. At the church there he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a>, king of Deheubarth, who had shortly before been driven from power by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caradog-ap-gruffydd">Caradog ap Gruffydd</a> of Glamorgan and Gwent, helped by Meilir ap Rhiwallon of Powys and Trahaearn. The two exiles made a pact and set out to give battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The armies met about a day's march north of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s. Gruffudd and Rhys came up with the forces of Trahaearn and Caradog in the evening; their opponents are reported to have had Norman crossbowmen in their army. According to his biography, Gruffudd insisted on attacking at once, overruling Rhys, who wished to wait until the next morning. The result was a complete victory in which all three leaders of the coalition were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Gruffudd took control of Gwynedd and, though later captured and imprisoned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> for some years, escaped and reigned until 1137. Rhys regained Deheubarth and ruled until 1093. Both were later prominent in Welsh resistance to the Normans, but in the short term the Normans profited from a struggle that had weakened all the major Welsh kingdoms. Later in 1081 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> became the first Norman king to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, marching across the south to St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s, where Rhys did homage and accepted his overlordship. Caradog's death left his kingdom in disarray and helped secure Norman dominance of the lowlands of Gwent and Glamorgan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Mynydd Carn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its last king, Caradog ap Gruffudd, was killed at the battle of <b>Mynydd Carn</b> in 1081, and Gwent became Norman marcher lordships, later Monmouthshire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Kingdom of Gwent</a></li></ul></section>
